        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;All things were made through Him&#x27; Cyril takes as a comprehensive denial of any creaturely status in the Son: the Word is not among things made, but is Himself the Maker. Cyril notes the deliberate Johannine choice of &#x27;di&#x27; autou&#x27; (through Him) rather than &#x27;hyp&#x27; autou&#x27; (by Him): the preposition does not subordinate the Son to the Father (as Arians read) but expresses the eternal Trinitarian order, the Father acting through the Son as His co-eternal Word. The Tewahedo Anaphora of the Apostles preserves this verse&#x27;s anti-Arian cadence in its prayer of consecration: &#x27;through whom all things were made&#x27;.</p></aside>',
