        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Orationes contra Arianos II.21</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;All things were made by him; and without him was not any thing made that was made&#x27; — Athanasius marks panta di&#x27; autou egeneto, kai chōris autou egeneto oude hen ho gegonen (all-things came-into-being THROUGH him, and apart-from-him came-into-being not-one-thing of-what-came-into-being) as the CREATIONAL-ASYMMETRY anchor against Arian inclusion-of-the-Son-in-creation. The Word is the THROUGH-WHOM-of-creation (di&#x27; autou), not part-of-what-came-into-being. The repeated egeneto / egeneto / gegonen (came-into-being) applies to creation-creatures, NEVER to the Word (Jn 1:1 — ēn ho Logos, was-the-Word, imperfect-tense-eternal-being, contrasts with egeneto came-into-being). The grammatical-distinction is decisive: the Word is, all-other-things came-to-be.</p></aside>',
