        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;In Him was life, and the life was the light of men&#x27; Cyril reads as the Son&#x27;s eternal possession of life — not life received, but life owned. The Word is the source of life for every creature that lives, and the life He bestows is also light: the rational illumination that distinguishes humanity. Cyril builds toward his pneumatological argument: this life-and-light shared by the Word with humanity is the prefiguring of the Spirit&#x27;s later indwelling. The Tewahedo baptismal liturgy preserves this connection: candidates are addressed as those returning to the Light-of-the-Word that was theirs in creation.</p></aside>',
