        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;The light shineth in darkness, and the darkness comprehended it not&#x27; Cyril reads as the post-lapsarian condition of humanity: the Word&#x27;s light continues to shine (creation has not extinguished it) but the darkness of human sin and demonic deception fails to receive it. The Greek &#x27;katelaben&#x27; carries the double sense of &#x27;comprehend&#x27; and &#x27;overcome&#x27; — Cyril preserves both: the darkness neither understands the Light nor masters it. The verse becomes for Cyril the Christological hinge: the Word continues to shine in every conscience even before the Incarnation, and continues afterward despite humanity&#x27;s refusal.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk I.5 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
