        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;He came unto His own, and His own received Him not&#x27; Cyril reads with characteristic precision: &#x27;His own&#x27; (ta idia) refers in the first sense to the Jewish people whom He had elected as His covenant possession, but in the broader sense to all humanity whom He had created. The double rejection (covenantal + creational) frames the Incarnation as the Maker&#x27;s return to a world that has forgotten Him. For Cyril this is no Marcionite cancellation of Israel but the painful pathos of God&#x27;s faithfulness meeting human refusal — the verse becomes the textual root of medieval Tewahedo homiletic readings of the prophetic ministry.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk I.11 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
