        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;As many as received Him, to them He gave the right (exousia) to become children of God&#x27; Cyril reads as the gift of adoptive sonship through participation in the natural Sonship of the Word. The &#x27;exousia&#x27; is not merely permission but operative power — divine grace that effects what it confers. Cyril here begins his exposition of theosis: humans become &#x27;children&#x27; (tekna, lower than &#x27;huios&#x27;) not by nature but by gift, through the Spirit&#x27;s adoption that conforms them to Christ&#x27;s image. This adoptive doctrine is foundational for the Tewahedo understanding of baptism as the moment of theosis-by-participation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk I.12 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
