        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Which were born, not of blood, nor of the will of the flesh, nor of the will of man, but of God&#x27; Cyril reads as the threefold negation of every natural-genealogical claim to divine sonship: Jewish ancestry (blood), human reproductive desire (will of flesh), patriarchal authority (will of man) all fail to constitute the new birth. Only the Spirit&#x27;s begetting through baptism produces children of God. Cyril notes that this verse is the conceptual seed of the later baptismal regeneration doctrine — the new birth is Trinitarian in structure (the Father&#x27;s gift, the Son&#x27;s mediation, the Spirit&#x27;s effecting) and ecclesial in setting (administered in the Church&#x27;s baptismal economy).</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk I.13 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
