    (
        1,
        51,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Hereafter ye shall see heaven open, and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of man&#x27; Cyril reads as the Christological fulfillment of Jacob&#x27;s ladder vision (Gen 28:12): the incarnate Christ IS the ladder uniting heaven and earth in His own Person. The angels&#x27; bidirectional movement signifies the restored communication between divinity and creation through the Word made flesh. Cyril notes that this is the first explicit messianic self-identification in John&#x27;s Gospel (&#x27;Son of man&#x27;) and that it grounds the entire subsequent series of theophanic &#x27;I am&#x27; sayings.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.51 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
