    (
        2,
        4,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>Cyril carefully defends Christ&#x27;s address to His mother at Cana (&#x27;Woman, what have I to do with thee?&#x27;) against any reading that would imply disrespect. &#x27;Woman&#x27; (gynai) is for Cyril a formal address of honor, not familiarity-refused; the question is rhetorical, situating the miracle within His Father&#x27;s appointed timing rather than maternal pressure. Mary&#x27;s authority remains intact (she immediately directs the servants to obey Christ) — the verse establishes the principle that Christ&#x27;s miracles proceed from the Father&#x27;s will, not human request, even when that human is His own mother. Tewahedo Marian veneration preserves this delicate balance.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.4 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
