    (
        4,
        14,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>Christ&#x27;s promise to the Samaritan woman — &#x27;Whosoever drinketh of the water that I shall give him shall never thirst; but the water that I shall give him shall be in him a well of water springing up into everlasting life&#x27; — Cyril reads pneumatologically: the water is the Holy Spirit Himself, given through Christ as His own gift to those who receive Him. The water&#x27;s spring inside the believer (pēgē hydatos hallomenou) figures the Spirit&#x27;s permanent indwelling, distinguished from the Old Covenant prophetic intermittent inspiration. &#x27;Springing up&#x27; (hallomenou) carries the sense of leaping, dynamic ascent — the Spirit&#x27;s indwelling is not static container but living source of theosis.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.14 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
