    (
        4,
        24,
        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;God is a Spirit&#x27; (pneuma ho theos) — Cyril reads this as ontological statement (the divine nature is spiritual, not material) and as missiological imperative (those who would worship a spiritual God must be spiritually transformed). Against the Anthropomorphites who attributed bodily form to God, Cyril insists the verse settles the question: God has no parts, no extension, no spatial location. Yet against any Hellenizing collapse of God into pure abstraction, Cyril preserves the personal-relational character: the Spirit-God is the Father, the Son, the Holy Spirit — three Persons in one spiritual essence. Worship &#x27;in spirit and in truth&#x27; is therefore the only worship corresponding to God&#x27;s actual mode of being.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.24 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
