    (
        4,
        34,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;My meat is to do the will of Him that sent me, and to finish His work&#x27; Cyril reads as Christ&#x27;s self-disclosure of the perfect harmony between His human will and the Father&#x27;s eternal will. Against the Apollinarian denial of Christ&#x27;s human soul (or against any reading that would make Christ&#x27;s will merely divine), Cyril here recognizes that Christ has a genuinely human will, but it is so perfectly conformed to the Father&#x27;s will that &#x27;doing the Father&#x27;s will&#x27; becomes His characteristic sustenance — what physical food is to bodily life, the doing of the divine will is to Christ&#x27;s incarnate human life. This is the seed of the dyothelite-anticipation that becomes explicit in later Greek theology, though Cyril himself does not yet use those terms.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.34 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
