    (
        4,
        42,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>The Samaritans&#x27; climactic confession — &#x27;This is indeed the Christ, the Saviour of the world&#x27; (ho sōtēr tou kosmou) — Cyril emphasizes as the proleptic Gentile recognition of Christ&#x27;s universal salvific scope. The Samaritans, themselves marginal to Jewish covenant identity, become the prototype for the Gentile inclusion that will mark the post-Pentecost Church. &#x27;Saviour of the world&#x27; is for Cyril a title both universal (Christ saves the world, not merely a portion) and particular (each person is saved as individual within that universal scope). The Tewahedo Synaxarium honors Photine (the traditional name of the Samaritan woman) on the 26th of Hidar — her witness to her village becomes the first apostolic missionary act recorded in John.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.42 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
