    (
        5,
        17,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;My Father worketh hitherto, and I work&#x27; (heōs arti) — Cyril reads as one of the most decisive anti-Arian sayings in the Gospel. The Sabbath rest of Gen 2:2 did not mean God ceased operating (providence is unbroken); Christ here claims the same uninterrupted operative power that belongs to the Father. The &#x27;and I&#x27; (kagō) refuses any subordination: the Son&#x27;s working is co-equal, co-eternal, co-extensive with the Father&#x27;s. The Jews&#x27; immediate reaction (5:18: they sought to kill Him for &#x27;making Himself equal with God&#x27;) confirms they understood the claim correctly. Cyril uses this verse against the Eusebian semi-Arians of his own day who would dilute the Son&#x27;s operative parity with the Father.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.17 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
