        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;As the Father hath life in Himself, so hath He given to the Son to have life in Himself&#x27; is for Cyril a critical text for the Son&#x27;s natural-not-derived divinity. The &#x27;given&#x27; (edōken) is not temporal grant (as if the Son did not always possess what He now has) but eternal communication of essence — the Father eternally communicates to the Son the same divine life He has from Himself. To &#x27;have life in Himself&#x27; (echein zōēn en heautō) is the divine attribute par excellence: not life received from elsewhere, but life as one&#x27;s own essence. The Father has it without source; the Son has it from the Father, yet equally and eternally. This is Cyril&#x27;s most precise statement of the Cappadocian-Athanasian doctrine of eternal generation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.26 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
