    (
        5,
        46,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;For had ye believed Moses, ye would have believed me: for he wrote of me&#x27; Cyril reads as Christ&#x27;s authoritative claim to be the proper object of every Mosaic prophecy. The verse establishes the principle that Moses and Christ stand in continuity, not discontinuity: rejection of Christ is rejection of Moses (and conversely, true reception of Moses leads to Christ). Cyril uses this verse against any Manichaean or Marcionite reading that would oppose the Testaments; he also uses it against any Jewish reading that would treat Mosaic obedience as sufficient apart from Christological reception. The Tewahedo Pentateuch readings in the Mäṣḥafä Qǝddase preserve this Cyrilline unity by always pairing Torah readings with their Christological NT fulfillment.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk II.46 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
