    (
        6,
        38,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;I came down from heaven, not to do mine own will, but the will of Him that sent me&#x27; Cyril reads against any reading that would set the Son&#x27;s will in opposition to the Father&#x27;s. The &#x27;not mine own&#x27; (ouch hina poiō) does not deny that the Son HAS a will (Apollinarian denial), nor does it imply imperfect alignment requiring submission. Rather, Cyril notes the Son&#x27;s INCARNATE human will is now in question: as man, the Son receives the Father&#x27;s will and acts upon it; as God, His will is identical with the Father&#x27;s by nature. This is one of Cyril&#x27;s earliest articulations of what later dyothelite theology would systematize: two wills (divine + human) in one Person, perfectly aligned.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk III.38 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
