    (
        6,
        44,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;No man can come to me, except the Father which hath sent me draw him&#x27; Cyril reads as the Johannine doctrine of prevenient grace. The Father&#x27;s &#x27;drawing&#x27; (helkysē) is the operative work of grace that precedes and enables human response to Christ; without it, no one comes. Cyril is careful here against Pelagian readings: the verse strictly excludes natural human capacity to come to Christ apart from divine initiative. Yet the &#x27;drawing&#x27; is not coercive — Cyril (following the classic patristic distinction) reads it as the Father&#x27;s drawing through the Spirit&#x27;s inward attraction, which the human will may still resist (against later Augustinian readings that would make it irresistible). This passage grounds the Eastern Christian doctrine of synergism: grace initiates, the will cooperates.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk III.44 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
