    (
        6,
        51,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;I am the living bread which came down from heaven: if any man eat of this bread, he shall live for ever: and the bread that I will give is my flesh, which I will give for the life of the world&#x27; is for Cyril the central eucharistic-Christological text in the entire Gospel. The bread Christ gives IS His own flesh — the personal Word&#x27;s true flesh, not a metaphor or symbol, but the incarnate body offered on the cross and now communicated through the eucharist. &#x27;For the life of the world&#x27; confirms the universal scope of the offering — not for the elect alone, not for the Jews alone, but for the kosmos. Cyril uses this verse as the textual foundation for his doctrine of eucharistic realism that becomes determinative for all subsequent Greek and Oriental Orthodox theology.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk III.51 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
