    (
        6,
        53,
        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Except ye eat the flesh of the Son of man, and drink His blood, ye have no life in you&#x27; Cyril takes with full sacramental seriousness: this is not metaphor but ontological condition. The eucharistic eating-and-drinking is the necessary means by which the life of the incarnate Word is communicated to the believer&#x27;s body and soul. Against any spiritualizing reading that would dissolve the verse into mere faith-receptive metaphor, Cyril insists the verse means what it says: the actual flesh and blood of Christ, sacramentally present, must be actually received. &#x27;Ye have no life in you&#x27; (ouk echete zōēn en heautois) is the privative consequence of refusing the eucharistic gift. This is the locus classicus of Cyril&#x27;s eucharistic theology that the Tewahedo Anaphora preserves verbatim.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk IV.53 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
