    (
        6,
        54,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Whoso eateth my flesh, and drinketh my blood, hath eternal life; and I will raise him up at the last day&#x27; Cyril reads as the eucharistic guarantee of bodily resurrection. The flesh of Christ communicated to the believer&#x27;s body becomes the seed of the resurrection body — Cyril&#x27;s standard formula is that the eucharist is the &#x27;medicine of immortality&#x27; (pharmakon athanasias, from Ignatius). The &#x27;I will raise him up&#x27; is Christ&#x27;s personal promise tied to the eucharistic reception: the body that has received the Body of Christ has within it the seed of eschatological transformation. This verse anchors Cyril&#x27;s distinctive contribution to eucharistic theology: the sacrament does not merely commemorate or symbolize, it actually communicates the resurrection life.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk IV.54 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
