    (
        6,
        55,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;For my flesh is meat indeed (alēthōs), and my blood is drink indeed&#x27; Cyril takes as decisive against any reading that would weaken eucharistic realism. The Greek &#x27;alēthōs&#x27; means &#x27;truly / really / actually&#x27; — Christ here explicitly disqualifies any metaphorical or symbolic interpretation of His preceding words. Against the gnostics of his own era and against any anticipatory spiritualizing readings, Cyril insists: TRULY flesh, REALLY blood — the eucharistic gift IS what it appears to be on the level of substance, even while remaining bread and wine to the senses. This is Cyril&#x27;s clearest articulation of what later Western scholasticism would call the doctrine of real presence (without scholastic categories of substance/accidents).</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk IV.55 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
