        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;If any man will do His will, he shall know of the doctrine, whether it be of God&#x27; Cyril takes as the dominical statement of moral epistemology: knowledge of divine truth is conditioned on willingness to obey. Against any purely intellectual approach to theological knowledge (which would make speculation independent of holiness), Cyril here grounds the patristic axiom: theology requires askesis. The &#x27;will to do His will&#x27; (thelē to thelēma autou poiein) Cyril emphasizes as primary disposition; the cognitive recognition follows on it. This verse becomes the textual root of the entire Eastern Orthodox tradition that knowledge of God presupposes purification of the nous through ascetic practice.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk IV.17 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
