    (
        7,
        37,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;In the last day, that great day of the feast, Jesus stood and cried, saying, If any man thirst, let him come unto me, and drink&#x27; Cyril notes the timing with care: on the last day of the Feast of Tabernacles, when the temple&#x27;s water-libation ceremony reached its climax, Christ stood up to offer Himself as the true reality the ceremony only prefigured. The water libation pleaded for the autumn rains; Christ offers the Living Water of the Spirit that gives eternal life. The &#x27;standing&#x27; (statheis) and &#x27;crying&#x27; (ekraxen) Cyril takes as proleptic posture of the eschatological Christ — standing as the judge, crying as the herald of salvation. The Tewahedo Mäṣḥafä Qǝddase Anaphora prayers for the Holy Spirit echo this passage&#x27;s invitation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk V.37 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
