    (
        7,
        46,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>The temple officers&#x27; report — &#x27;Never man spake like this Man&#x27; (oudepote elalēsen houtōs anthrōpos) — Cyril takes as inspired witness from unwilling sources. The very men sent to arrest Christ become the heralds of His unique authority: they cannot perform their task because the sheer weight of His word disarms them. Cyril notes the linguistic precision: not merely &#x27;never has anyone spoken&#x27; but specifically &#x27;never man&#x27; (anthrōpos) — implying recognition that more than a mere man speaks here. The verse becomes for Cyril the historical-witness confirmation of the Logos&#x27;s incarnate authority — even those resistant to faith bear unintended testimony to it.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk V.46 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
