    (
        11,
        26,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Whosoever liveth and believeth in me shall never die&#x27; Cyril treats with careful eschatological precision: &#x27;never die&#x27; (ou mē apothanē eis ton aiōna) does NOT deny the universal experience of bodily death (which Christians clearly continue to experience) but denies death&#x27;s POWER as a permanent severance from God. The believer dies physically but is not dead spiritually; the body sleeps but the soul lives in Christ; the resurrection at the last day reunites and consummates. This realized-eschatology reading is paired with the future-eschatology of v. 24-25; Cyril holds both together as the proper Christian doctrine of death and resurrection — fully realized in part now, awaiting full consummation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk VII.26 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
