    (
        11,
        40,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Said I not unto thee, that, if thou wouldest believe, thou shouldest see the glory of God&#x27; Cyril reads as the principle that faith is the epistemic condition for perceiving divine glory. Martha&#x27;s prior hesitation (&#x27;by this time he stinketh&#x27;) is gently rebuked; Christ reframes the situation: belief is not response to the miracle but PRECONDITION for properly perceiving it. The &#x27;glory of God&#x27; Cyril takes here as denoting both the resurrection-miracle about to occur AND the eternal Glory of the Son which the miracle makes visible. The Tewahedo liturgical posture before consecration — the faithful stand in reverent silence, the deacon calls &#x27;Let us attend&#x27; — embodies this Cyrilline principle: faith first, vision second.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk VII.40 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
