    (
        11,
        41,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>Christ&#x27;s prayer of thanksgiving — &#x27;Father, I thank thee that thou hast heard me&#x27; — Cyril carefully defends against any subordinationist reading. The prayer is not informational request (as if the Son needed to ask the Father for power) but liturgical-pedagogical: spoken for the benefit of the bystanders to manifest the unbroken Trinitarian communion. &#x27;Thou hast heard me&#x27; (in the perfect tense — ēkousas) Cyril reads as evidencing the eternal mutual-knowing of Father and Son: the Father has eternally &#x27;heard&#x27; because the Son and Father share one divine knowledge. The prayer is the incarnate Word&#x27;s pedagogical disclosure of what is always already eternally true.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk VII.41 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
