    (
        11,
        43,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Lazarus, come forth&#x27; (Lazare, deuro exō) — Cyril notes the cosmic significance of the imperative addressed to a corpse. The verb &#x27;come forth&#x27; presupposes a hearer capable of obeying; Christ speaks to Lazarus as already living, since His Word IS life-giving. The naming &#x27;Lazarus&#x27; Cyril takes as theologically precise: Christ calls only this one out of the tomb (else, the patristic tradition observes, all the dead would have risen). This personal-specific calling prefigures the eschatological resurrection where each believer is called by name. The Tewahedo Easter Anaphora&#x27;s prayer &#x27;arise, my soul, from the dust&#x27; explicitly echoes this Lazarus pattern.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk VII.43 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
