    (
        12,
        24,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Except a corn of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abideth alone: but if it die, it bringeth forth much fruit&#x27; Cyril reads at multiple levels simultaneously. Most literally, it is Christ&#x27;s self-prediction: His death is the necessary condition for the harvest of redemption. Typologically, it prefigures every Christian death-and-resurrection (baptism as participation in His pattern). Pneumatologically, the fruit-bearing is the Spirit&#x27;s work in the Church proceeding from the cross. Cyril notes the grammatical force: not &#x27;IF it die&#x27; (uncertain) but &#x27;IF it die&#x27; (conditional contrast) — death is the NECESSARY price of the harvest, not an unfortunate possibility. This verse anchors Cyril&#x27;s theology of redemptive suffering both for Christ and for those united to Him.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk VIII.24 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
