    (
        13,
        35,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another&#x27; Cyril takes as Christ&#x27;s missiological-apologetic instruction: the visible love-community of the Church is the primary evidential basis for the world&#x27;s recognition of Christian truth. Not signs, not eloquence, not philosophical sophistication — LOVE for one another. &#x27;All men&#x27; (pantes) Cyril reads as universal scope: this evidential love is not for in-house consumption but for the cosmos to witness. The Tewahedo monastic tradition preserves this verse as the controlling text for community life: the Synaxarium repeatedly notes saints whose distinguishing mark was conspicuous love for the brethren and the poor.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk IX.35 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
