    (
        14,
        10,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;I am in the Father, and the Father in me&#x27; (egō en tō Patri, kai ho Patēr en emoi) is for Cyril the foundational Johannine text for what later Greek theology would call perichoresis: the mutual indwelling of the Persons of the Trinity. The reciprocal &#x27;in&#x27; (en) preserves the Personal distinction (the Son is not the Father, the Father is not the Son) while affirming the consubstantial unity (each is fully in the other by virtue of the one shared divine essence). &#x27;The words that I speak unto you, I speak not of myself&#x27; (continuing the verse) Cyril reads not as denial of personal agency but as expressing the unbroken Trinitarian operation: the Father&#x27;s words come through the Son in the Spirit. This verse is the textual foundation of Cyril&#x27;s Trinitarian theology.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk IX.10 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
