    (
        16,
        13,
        "e",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;When He, the Spirit of truth, is come, He will guide you into all truth: for He shall not speak of Himself; but whatsoever He shall hear, that shall He speak&#x27; Cyril unpacks against any reading that would make the Spirit a separate or supplementary teaching source. &#x27;He shall not speak of Himself&#x27; Cyril reads as expressing perfect Trinitarian harmony: the Spirit&#x27;s teaching IS the Father&#x27;s + Son&#x27;s teaching, mediated through the Spirit&#x27;s distinctive operation, not a separate revelation. &#x27;All truth&#x27; (eis tēn alētheian pasan) Cyril limits to the Christological-soteriological deposit: the Spirit guides into ALL the truth Christ has given, not into endless new revelations. This grounds patristic-Tewahedo apostolic-deposit doctrine.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk XI.13 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
    (
        16,
        13,
        "f",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Epistulae ad Serapionem 1.20</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Howbeit when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth: for he shall not speak of himself; but whatsoever he shall hear, that shall he speak: and he will shew you things to come&#x27; — Athanasius marks the SPIRIT-OF-TRUTH ECONOMIC-MISSION as the Trinitarian-economy-of-revelation: the Spirit is sent-by-the-Son (who-is-the-truth, Jn 14:6), receives-from-the-Son (everything he speaks is from-the-Son), and guides-believers-into-the-Son. The Spirit&#x27;s-procession-and-mission is Christo-centric in its economic-form. Jn 16:13 is the principal Johannine anchor for the doctrine that Letters to Serapion expounds: the Spirit, though distinct-Person from Father and Son, is homoousios with both and stands in unbroken-economic-coordination.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Epistulae ad Serapionem de Spiritu Sancto (Letters to Serapion on the Holy Spirit, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857).",
    ),
