    (
        16,
        20,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Ye shall be sorrowful, but your sorrow shall be turned into joy&#x27; Cyril reads as the eschatological pattern of Christian experience: present suffering is not negated but transmuted by the resurrection. &#x27;Turned&#x27; (genēsetai eis charan — shall be made into joy) Cyril takes literally: not &#x27;replaced by&#x27; joy after sorrow ends, but TRANSFORMED INTO joy. The Easter pattern (Friday&#x27;s sorrow becomes Sunday&#x27;s joy through the cross-resurrection event) is the paradigm. Cyril applies this to every subsequent Christian experience of suffering: nothing in the Christian life is finally lost; every present sorrow is the seed of a future joy by participation in the paschal pattern. The Tewahedo Easter Anaphora preserves this transformative reading.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk XI.20 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
