    (
        18,
        38,
        "b",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>Pilate&#x27;s question — &#x27;What is truth?&#x27; (ti estin alētheia) — Cyril treats with characteristic theological tragedy. Truth Himself stands before Pilate (Christ is the truth per 14:6); Pilate fails to recognize what is right in front of him. Cyril notes the absence of any answer from Christ: the only proper response to a question that misses what is already self-evidently present is silence. Pilate&#x27;s tragic moment becomes the prototype of every subsequent encounter with Christ where the questioner cannot or will not see what is before them. The Tewahedo Good Friday liturgy preserves this moment with particular weight; the question and the silence stand as the textual icon of unbelief.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk XII.38 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
