    (
        19,
        30,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>Christ&#x27;s dying word — &#x27;It is finished&#x27; (tetelestai) — Cyril takes as the dominical declaration of completed accomplishment. The perfect tense (tetelestai — completed action with continuing result) Cyril emphasizes: the work IS finished, definitively and abidingly. NOT &#x27;I am dying&#x27; or &#x27;I have failed&#x27; but &#x27;the work is complete.&#x27; Cyril reads this as encompassing: the entire prophetic-typological economy of the Old Testament now fulfilled; the redemptive work for which the Word became flesh now consummated; the price for sin now paid in full. &#x27;He bowed His head, and gave up the ghost&#x27; Cyril notes the order: Christ acts (bows head, gives spirit) — He is not passively overtaken by death but actively releases His life. The cross is sovereign self-offering, not victim&#x27;s defeat.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk XII.30 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
