    (
        20,
        29,
        "d",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (428).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on the Gospel of John</em> <small>(c. 428 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Thomas, because thou hast seen me, thou hast believed: blessed are they that have not seen, and yet have believed&#x27; Cyril takes as the dominical pin for faith-without-direct-evidence as the normative Christian condition. The Risen Christ does NOT rebuke Thomas&#x27;s prior demand for evidence (the wounds are shown, the touch is offered); but He does proleptically commend the future generations whose faith will rest on apostolic testimony rather than direct sensory verification. The &#x27;blessed&#x27; (makarioi) Cyril reads as ranking apostolic-testimony-based faith on the same scale as direct-sight faith — perhaps even higher, since it operates with less crutch. This grounds the entire Tewahedo catechetical-apologetic tradition that proclaims the Gospel as worthy of faith based on apostolic testimony.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on John, Bk XII.29 (NPNF S2 V14). PD.",
    ),
